Wrong. 4+5 implies that *combat* using arms and legs isn't affected by
being held. You can't say that any activity involving arms or legs
that is more complicated than physical combat (like painting a fresco
on the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel or casting a difficult spell)
aren't affected either. That's an unwarranted generalization on a
special case that is far simpler.

Now, given 1+3+5 it follows that magic requires more effort than
combat (notice you omitted the asumption that magic requires use of
hands). If the reason is really that casting spells requires you to
wave your hands in a more complicated fashion than physical combat,
then it is foreseeable that being held could potentially affect
spellcasting even when it doesn't affect physical combat

>Deduction (c, from a and b): being held doesn't affect spell casting (QED).

